eveloped land to develop into developed lots for the construction of single-family homes, as well as for sale to others. Further, it originates and sells mortgages; and serves as a title insurance agent by providing title insurance policies, examination, and closing services to purchasers of its homes. The company was formerly known as M/I Schottenstein Homes, Inc. and changed its name to M/I Homes, Inc. in January 2004. M/I Homes, Inc. was founded in 1976 and is based in Columbus, Ohio.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Robert H. Schottenstein J.D. | Chairman, President & CEO | Age 72 | Compensation: $5.0M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 1K people. Revenue per employee of $2.5M ranks in the top quartile among Consumer Cyclical peers, improving at 16%/yr \u2014 growing workforce leverage. Each employee generates $71K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $9K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 2\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (3/10).","financial_summary":["M/I Homes, Inc. is a mid-cap consumer cyclical business with a $3.3B market cap on $4.4B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +2% over the past three years and +12% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 20% against a 10% cost of capital \u2014 a 9-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat.","The business throws off $128M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 3 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders. Cash conversion is thin at 0.34\u00d7, however, which raises the oldest question in analysis: are these earnings backed by cash?.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.0\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA.","Management returns a meaningful 6.1% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insiders hold 2.2% of the shares."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 112% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 1 other valuation measure)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":1.1176882865491953},{"text":"Piotroski score 2/9 \u2014 the fundamentals are deteriorating on multiple fronts","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 7.2% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $11.1M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Financial quality metrics lag Consumer Cyclical peers","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Cyclical sectors like Consumer Cyclical tend to underperform in slowdowns","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Consumer staples outperforming discretionary, signaling cautious spending","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Above-average market volatility (VIX 26)","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Brand relevance decay \u2014 shifting consumer preferences and social-media-driven trends can strand inventory and invalidate positioning faster than product cycles can adapt.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk","Consumer Cyclical sector outperforming the broader market over the past quarter"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"M/I Homes (MHO) Faces Softer Earnings While Fair Value Still Suggests Upside","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://simplywall.st/stocks/us/consumer-durables/nyse-mho/m-i-homes/news/mi-homes-mho-faces-softer-earnings-while-fair-value-still-su","date":"2026-11-01","timestamp":1793557292.914,"origin":"tiingo","sentime